768
01:15:55,182 --> 01:16:02,084
First, how to design intelligent agents that can reason, use tools, and even improve themselves over time.

769
01:16:02,446 --> 01:16:11,280
And second, how to handle one of the biggest challenges in AI, that is, hallucinations, and ensure our systems provide trustworthy,

770
01:16:11,440 --> 01:16:13,040
verifiable information.

771
01:16:13,454 --> 01:16:14,564
Let's dive right in.

772
01:16:14,990 --> 01:16:23,268
To begin with, let's talk about what Langchain agents are and why they represent a significant leap forward in AI application development.

773
01:16:23,854 --> 01:16:31,428
First and foremost, agents are autonomous decision makers that use LLMs to determine which actions to take and in what order.

774
01:16:31,982 --> 01:16:40,196
Unlike the chains we discussed earlier, where the workflow is predefined, agents think for themselves and chart their own path to solve problems.

775
01:16:40,462 --> 01:16:48,400
Second, they are tool-equipped systems that dynamically access search engines, calculators, databases, and APS

776
01:16:48,720 --> 01:16:49,956
to complete tasks.

777
01:16:50,510 --> 01:16:53,668
Think of agents as having a toolbox at their disposal.

778
01:16:54,062 --> 01:16:59,428
They can reach for whatever tool they need whenever they need it based on the situations at hand.

779
01:16:59,918 --> 01:17:09,600
Third, agents are iterative problem solvers that observe action results, reason about outcomes, and decide next steps until task completion.

780
01:17:09,934 --> 01:17:16,804
They don't just execute and finish, they evaluate whether they are making progress and adapt their approach accordingly.

781
01:17:17,230 --> 01:17:21,732
Most importantly, agents are dynamic and adaptive, unlike chains.

782
01:17:22,414 --> 01:17:31,140
While chains follow a predetermined path, agents assess situations in real time and adjust their strategies based on results.

783
01:17:32,014 --> 01:17:40,132
If one approach isn't working, they pivot and try something different, much like how a human would tackle an unfamiliar problem.

784
01:17:41,006 --> 01:17:52,196
This autonomous, adaptive capability makes agents incredibly powerful for complex, unpredictable scenarios where a fixed workflow simply won't suffice.

785
01:17:53,038 --> 01:17:57,444
They bring true intelligence and flexibility to your AI applications.

786
01:17:58,126 --> 01:18:03,492
So, let's now understand the fundamentals of how AI agents actually work.

787
01:18:04,238 --> 01:18:09,252
If you have ever watched a person solve a difficult problem, you have seen this pattern.

788
01:18:09,902 --> 01:18:17,380
They observe the situation, think about what to do, take action, see what happens, and then repeat.

789
01:18:17,742 --> 01:18:20,452
AI agents work exactly the same way.

790
01:18:21,102 --> 01:18:23,012
This is called the reasoning loop.

791
01:18:23,760 --> 01:18:26,820
Observe, think, act, and repeat.

792
01:18:27,470 --> 01:18:29,508
It's continuous and dynamic.

793
01:18:30,062 --> 01:18:36,420
The agent isn't just executing a pre-programmed script, it's adapting based on what it encounters.

794
01:18:36,640 --> 01:18:44,228
Now, the most popular implementation of this is the React pattern, which stands for reasoning and acting.

795
01:18:44,942 --> 01:18:46,116
This is how it flows.

796
01:18:46,926 --> 01:18:56,068
The agent generates a thought about what it needs to do, decides on action to take, maybe calling a tool or querying a database,

797
01:18:56,750 --> 01:18:59,460
then receives an observation about what happened.

798
01:19:00,430 --> 01:19:05,316
Based on that observation, it generates another thought and the cycle continues.

799
01:19:06,446 --> 01:19:08,740
But agents need memory to be effective.

800
01:19:09,198 --> 01:19:11,204
We work with two layers here.

801
01:19:11,694 --> 01:19:18,372
Short-term memory is your context window, the immediate conversation history the agent is currently working with.

802
01:19:18,958 --> 01:19:26,404
Then long-term memory uses vector stores to retrieve relevant information from past interactions or knowledge bases.

803
01:19:27,150 --> 01:19:32,420
Think of short-term as your working memory and long-term as your reference library.

804
01:19:32,814 --> 01:19:35,012
And critically, we need guardrails.

805
01:19:35,534 --> 01:19:39,172
Before the agent processes anything, we validate inputs.

806
01:19:39,918 --> 01:19:41,348
Is this request safe?

807
01:19:41,678 --> 01:19:43,300
Is it properly formatted?

808
01:19:43,822 --> 01:19:50,916
And after the agent generates output, we process it carefully to ensure it is structured correctly and makes sense.

809
01:19:51,630 --> 01:19:54,980
These guardrails prevent your agent from going off the rails.

810
01:19:55,534 --> 01:20:03,524
Now that we understand the core architecture, let's talk about what it takes to actually build agents that work reliably in production.

811
01:20:03,982 --> 01:20:11,556
When you're building an agent, there are three non-negotiables that separate robust production systems from fragile prototypes.

812
01:20:12,302 --> 01:20:14,692
First, clear tool schemas.

813
01:20:15,182 --> 01:20:19,296
Every tool your agent can use needs a crystal clear definition.

814
01:20:19,566 --> 01:20:21,188
What parameters does it accept?

815
01:20:21,934 --> 01:20:24,196
What format does it return data in?

816
01:20:24,878 --> 01:20:26,084
What can go wrong?

817
01:20:26,702 --> 01:20:31,396
If your agent has access to a weather API, the schema should specify.

818
01:20:32,206 --> 01:20:39,044
This tool takes a location string and returns temperature, conditions, and forecast as JSON.

819
01:20:39,536 --> 01:20:40,740
No ambiguity.

820
01:20:41,358 --> 01:20:48,016
When your LLM understands exactly what each tool does, it makes better decisions about when to use them.

821
01:20:48,398 --> 01:20:50,724
Second, fallback reasoning paths.

822
01:20:51,342 --> 01:20:52,576
Things will go wrong.

823
01:20:52,846 --> 01:20:57,188
API is timeout, databases are temporarily unavailable.

824
01:20:57,806 --> 01:21:00,196
The agent needs predefined backup plans.

825
01:21:00,750 --> 01:21:05,092
If the primary search tool fails, maybe it tries a secondary source.

826
01:21:05,774 --> 01:21:13,284
If a calculation tool errors out, maybe it attempts a simpler approximation or asks the user for clarification.

827
01:21:14,094 --> 01:21:20,068
The worst thing that can happen is your agent just stops and returns an error message with no alternative.

828
01:21:20,590 --> 01:21:23,044
Third, human in the loop triggers.

829
01:21:23,758 --> 01:21:28,676
Some decisions are too important or too risky to leave entirely to AI.

830
01:21:29,294 --> 01:21:36,356
You need to define clear thresholds where the agent is about to delete data, pause, and ask for confirmation.

831
01:21:37,102 --> 01:21:42,212
If it's uncertain about a medical diagnosis, escalate to a human expert.

832
01:21:42,862 --> 01:21:47,940
If it's making a financial transaction above a certain amount, require approval.

833
01:21:48,590 --> 01:21:54,212
These triggers ensure your agent is assistive, not autonomous in dangerous ways.

