01:28:55,118 --> 01:29:01,092
Even the most sophisticated agent architecture cannot overcome fundamental challenges with LLMs.

901
01:29:01,806 --> 01:29:10,020
So, hallucination is the single biggest obstacle to deploying LLMs in production, and it affects every agent we have discussed,

902
01:29:10,192 --> 01:29:12,228
regardless of how well designed they are.

903
01:29:12,750 --> 01:29:14,820
So, what exactly are hallucinations?

904
01:29:15,438 --> 01:29:18,020
They are confident but completely wrong outputs.

905
01:29:18,894 --> 01:29:29,220
LLM generates the text that sounds authoritative, well-reasoned, and plausible, but it's factually incorrect or entirely fabricated.

906
01:29:29,646 --> 01:29:32,004
Let me give you some sobering statistics.

907
01:29:32,686 --> 01:29:39,812
In long-form responses, studies show that up to 80% can contain at least one fabricated fact.

908
01:29:40,654 --> 01:29:45,872
For knowledge-intensive tasks, error rates sit between 30% to 50%.

909
01:29:46,734 --> 01:29:56,912
And when you ask the model to do multi-hop reasoning where it needs to combine multiple pieces of information to reach a conclusion, error rates jump above 70%.

910
01:29:57,710 --> 01:29:58,820
Why does this happen?

911
01:29:59,374 --> 01:30:01,124
There are three root causes.

912
01:30:01,678 --> 01:30:03,364
First is training data gaps.

913
01:30:04,142 --> 01:30:07,364
The model was trained on data up to a certain cutoff rate.

914
01:30:07,886 --> 01:30:10,404
Anything after that, the model has to guess.

915
01:30:11,086 --> 01:30:19,396
Even for topics within its training data, if something was underrepresented, the model lacks strong signal and starts making things up.

916
01:30:19,918 --> 01:30:21,988
Second is context window limits.

917
01:30:22,702 --> 01:30:29,632
Even with modern models that support 100,000 to 200,000 tokens, you can't fit everything.

918
01:30:29,902 --> 01:30:36,452
If the relevant information isn't in the context, the model fills in the blanks, often incorrectly.

919
01:30:37,326 --> 01:30:43,332
Third, and this is a fundamental to how LLMs work, overconfident token prediction.

920
01:30:43,982 --> 01:30:48,484
The model is predicting the next most likely token based on patterns it learned.

921
01:30:49,006 --> 01:30:53,764
It doesn't have a built in mechanism to say, I don't know, when it's uncertain.

922
01:30:54,222 --> 01:31:00,420
It just generates whatever seems more probable, even if that probability is based on shaky ground.

