02:12:10,542 --> 02:12:13,924
Rewrite the conclusion in a more confident, persuasive tone.

1419
02:12:14,798 --> 02:12:21,764
Full document polish, review the entire piece for clarity and consistency, and highlight anything that feels weak.

1420
02:12:22,446 --> 02:12:32,164
Every change happens within the document itself, keeping your work clean, visible, and trackable rather than fragmented across a long conversation thread.

1421
02:12:32,878 --> 02:12:35,264
Last step is nothing but export when ready.

1422
02:12:35,438 --> 02:12:42,148
Once your document reaches the standard you need, export it to Word or Google Docs for final formatting.

1423
02:12:43,054 --> 02:12:45,860
Citation work or submission preparation.

1424
02:12:46,670 --> 02:12:48,356
Web search, what is it?

1425
02:12:48,750 --> 02:12:49,828
Why does it matter?

1426
02:12:50,958 --> 02:12:59,300
Let's go back to that second scenario from our introduction because it points to something fundamental about how AI models work and why it matters.

1427
02:13:00,142 --> 02:13:07,172
Every language model, including ChatGPT, is trained on data collected up to a specific point in time.

1428
02:13:07,694 --> 02:13:09,572
That's called its knowledge cutoff.

1429
02:13:10,190 --> 02:13:13,156
Everything the model knows, it learned before that day.

1430
02:13:13,902 --> 02:13:18,276
After that point, as far as the model is concerned, nothing has happened.

1431
02:13:18,990 --> 02:13:24,100
No news, no new research, no updated prices, no recent events.

1432
02:13:25,102 --> 02:13:29,380
The world stopped from its perspective at a fixed moment in the past.

1433
02:13:30,318 --> 02:13:32,708
For many things, that's completely fine.

1434
02:13:33,102 --> 02:13:40,484
Explaining a concept, helping you write, working through a logic problem, none of that meets today's headlines.

1435
02:13:41,486 --> 02:13:49,188
But the moment your question involves anything current, what's happening in the news right now, what a stock is trending at today,

1436
02:13:49,742 --> 02:13:57,444
whether it's going to rain tomorrow, or what changed in a policy last month, the default model simply cannot help you accurately.

1437
02:13:57,966 --> 02:14:00,036
Web search changes that entirely.

1438
02:14:00,622 --> 02:14:04,484
Websearch connects Chat GPT to the live internet in real time.

1439
02:14:05,390 --> 02:14:14,832
When you ask a question that needs current information, it goes to and searches the web, retrieves what's relevant, and brings it back into your conversation,

1440
02:14:15,150 --> 02:14:17,860
giving you answers grounded in what's true today.

1441
02:14:18,158 --> 02:14:21,316
But here's the important distinction from just switching to Google.

1442
02:14:21,998 --> 02:14:24,964
When you use a search engine, you get a list of links.

1443
02:14:25,646 --> 02:14:33,444
You still have to open each one, read through the content, figure out which source is most reliable, extract what's relevant,

1444
02:14:33,774 --> 02:14:35,556
and piece together your own answer.

1445
02:14:36,142 --> 02:14:40,036
When ChatGPT uses Web Search, it does all of that for you.

1446
02:14:40,718 --> 02:14:49,316
It retrieves the information, evaluates it, and delivers a clear, direct conversational answer that already incorporates what it found.

1447
02:14:50,126 --> 02:14:58,116
You will get the real-time reach of a search engine combined with the language intelligence of an AI that knows how to make sense of what it finds.

1448
02:14:59,278 --> 02:15:01,604
Web search adds the most value when you need.

1449
02:15:02,062 --> 02:15:03,684
Breaking news and current events.

1450
02:15:04,750 --> 02:15:07,072
What happened at the G20 summit this

1451
02:15:07,072 --> 02:15:09,156
week? Live data and figures.

1452
02:15:09,870 --> 02:15:13,252
What is the USD to INR exchange rate right now?

1453
02:15:13,934 --> 02:15:14,884
Weather forecasts.

1454
02:15:15,854 --> 02:15:17,924
What's the weather in Bangalore this weekend?

1455
02:15:18,606 --> 02:15:20,676
Recent product or technology updates.

1456
02:15:21,422 --> 02:15:23,844
What are the new features in the latest Android release?

1457
02:15:24,878 --> 02:15:35,268
Fast moving topics, regulatory changes, market movements, election outcomes, sports results, anything where last month's answers might already be wrong.

1458
02:15:35,790 --> 02:15:39,364
Web search versus deep research, knowing which to use.

1459
02:15:40,398 --> 02:15:43,940
Since both tools involve going to the internet, it's a fair question.

1460
02:15:44,846 --> 02:15:46,436
When do you use one versus?

1461
02:15:47,310 --> 02:15:47,792
The other?

1462
02:15:48,430 --> 02:15:56,068
Web search is used for quick current facts and live data, whereas deep research is used for complex multi-source analysis and reports.

1463
02:15:56,526 --> 02:16:00,036
When it comes to speed, web search is fast in results.

1464
02:16:00,526 --> 02:16:05,700
It gives results in seconds, but deep research is slower but more thorough by design.

1465
02:16:06,478 --> 02:16:16,420
When it comes to output style, in web search it is concise and it is a direct answer, but in deep research it is structured synthesis across multiple sources.

1466
02:16:17,262 --> 02:16:28,292
Web search is ideal for use cases like weather, prices, recent news, quick lookups, etc., whereas deep research comes into the picture in policy comparisons,

1467
02:16:28,592 --> 02:16:30,980
academic research, and market analysis.

1468
02:16:31,502 --> 02:16:32,644
What to keep in mind?

1469
02:16:33,006 --> 02:16:35,332
It requires Plus or Enterprise.

1470
02:16:35,854 --> 02:16:38,724
Web browsing is not available on the free plan.

1471
02:16:39,246 --> 02:16:44,932
If you are on a free account and need current information, upgrading to Plus is the most direct path.

1472
02:16:45,518 --> 02:16:47,716
Paywalled content is inaccessible.

1473
02:16:48,238 --> 02:16:54,660
Subscription based news outlets, academic journals behind paywalls, and similar sources cannot be accessed.

1474
02:16:55,662 --> 02:16:58,980
WebSear works with publicly available information only,

1475
02:16:59,662 --> 02:17:02,212
faster but less comprehensive than deep research.

1476
02:17:03,214 --> 02:17:06,052
Web search is optimized for speed and directness.

1477
02:17:06,830 --> 02:17:12,612
For nuanced, multidimensional topics that need thorough analysis, deep research will serve you better.

1478
02:17:13,262 --> 02:17:19,716
Verify high-stakes information for important decisions, medical, financial, legal.

1479
02:17:20,558 --> 02:17:24,772
Always cross-check what web search returns with authoritative primary sources.

1480
02:17:25,294 --> 02:17:29,904
AI summarizes well, but critical decisions deserve direct verification.

1481
02:17:30,222 --> 02:17:40,624
Think about the last time you had an idea in your head: a vivid scene, a concept, a visual you wanted to show someone but couldn't bring it to life because you didn't know how to draw,

1482
02:17:40,864 --> 02:17:42,980
design, or even edit the video.

1483
02:17:43,758 --> 02:17:56,612
Maybe you needed a product mock-up, a thumbnail for a presentation, a short clip to explain an idea, and the gap between what you imagined and what you could actually create felt frustratingly wide.

1484
02:17:57,454 --> 02:17:59,524
What if that gap no longer existed?

1485
02:18:00,046 --> 02:18:09,636
What if you could simply describe what you see in your mind in plain language, and an AI would build it for you in seconds as a polished image or a moving video?

1486
02:18:10,542 --> 02:18:12,228
That's not science fiction anymore.

1487
02:18:12,686 --> 02:18:17,444
That's exactly what today's AI image and video generation tools make possible.

1488
02:18:17,870 --> 02:18:20,388
What AI image and video generation is.

1489
02:18:21,358 --> 02:18:29,892
AI image and video generation refers to a category of tools that can create both images and videos directly from text prompts.

1490
02:18:30,894 --> 02:18:32,644
Let's unpack that in plain terms.

1491
02:18:33,230 --> 02:18:37,668
These tools are multimodal, meaning they work across multiple types of output.

1492
02:18:38,318 --> 02:18:40,516
You give them language, they give you imagery.

1493
02:18:41,070 --> 02:18:49,252
Think of them as highly skilled digital artists, ones that have studied millions of images, films, paintings, and photographs,

1494
02:18:49,870 --> 02:18:55,076
and can now take your written description and turn it into a visual that matches your intent.

1495
02:18:55,982 --> 02:19:04,644
You type a quiet fishing village at dawn, fog rolling over the water, warm orange light breaking through the clouds.

1496
02:19:05,710 --> 02:19:14,144
The model reads that and builds it, either as a stunning still image or as a short cinematic video clip with motion,

1497
02:19:14,384 --> 02:19:15,652
light, and atmosphere.

1498
02:19:16,224 --> 02:19:20,036
No design skills, no software training, just your words.

1499
02:19:20,846 --> 02:19:22,624
You may have heard of DAL E3.

1500
02:19:23,214 --> 02:19:34,980
It is currently the primary image generation tool built directly into Chat GPT, and it remains one of the most accessible and widely used image generation tools available today.

1501
02:19:35,918 --> 02:19:46,308
Separately, the field has expanded into video generation, a fundamentally harder challenge because generating video means producing hundreds of consistent frames,

1502
02:19:46,784 --> 02:19:47,556
not just one.

1503
02:19:47,918 --> 02:19:57,380
The AI doesn't just need to make something that looks right, it needs to make something that moves right with consistent lighting, physics, and motion across time.

1504
02:19:58,318 --> 02:20:06,436
These are two distinct and parallel tracks, image generation and video generation, and both have continued to evolve rapidly.

1505
02:20:06,990 --> 02:20:10,564
How does AI image generation actually work behind the scenes?

1506
02:20:11,566 --> 02:20:17,092
Let's look at what happens from the moment you type your prompt to the moment your visual appears.

1507
02:20:17,742 --> 02:20:19,908
The first one is the scene understanding.

1508
02:20:20,654 --> 02:20:26,532
The first thing the model does is read your prompt carefully and break it down into its components.

1509
02:20:27,502 --> 02:20:28,868
Take this example prompt

1510
02:20:29,678 --> 02:20:36,964
a futuristic city skyline at sunset, cyberpunk style, neon reflections on wet streets.

1511
02:20:38,318 --> 02:20:46,416
The model identifies the setting, a city skyline, the time and lighting, sunset, neon lights, reflections,

1512
02:20:46,798 --> 02:20:52,228
and the style being cyberpunk, which implies specific colors, textures, and mood.

1513
02:20:52,942 --> 02:20:58,724
The atmospheric detail, wet streets, amplified rain, reflections on surfaces.

1514
02:20:59,406 --> 02:21:07,748
It's not just reading keywords, it's building a rich internal representation of the scene before a single pixel is generated.

1515
02:21:08,686 --> 02:21:13,060
The second step is generation, how the image is actually built.

1516
02:21:13,902 --> 02:21:22,468
Here's where it gets interesting, and if you followed the Transformer Architecture section earlier in this course, you will recognize something familiar.

1517
02:21:23,118 --> 02:21:28,964
Modern AI image generation models, including DAO E3, use a process called diffusion.

1518
02:21:29,358 --> 02:21:35,300
Rather than drawing an image from scratch the way a human artist would, the model starts from the opposite end.

1519
02:21:35,982 --> 02:21:44,964
It begins with what is essentially pure random noise, a completely unstructured field of pixels, and then works backwards step by step,

1520
02:21:45,422 --> 02:21:51,108
removing the noise and progressively refining the image until something coherent and meaningful emerges.

1521
02:21:52,494 --> 02:21:55,460
Each refinement step is guided by your text prompt.

1522
02:21:56,078 --> 02:22:01,156
So at every stage the model is asking, does this look more like what was described?

1523
02:22:01,710 --> 02:22:04,324
And it keeps adjusting until the answer is yes.

1524
02:22:05,166 --> 02:22:09,444
The component doing that refinement, the denoiser, is a transformer.

1525
02:22:09,838 --> 02:22:15,364
So the same architecture that processes language in ChatGPT is also in a different form.

1526
02:22:15,822 --> 02:22:17,988
What shapes your image into existence?

1527
02:22:18,478 --> 02:22:22,660
That's why these models are sometimes called diffusion transformers.

1528
02:22:23,534 --> 02:22:28,580
They combine the diffusion process with the transformer architecture you already know.

1529
02:22:29,646 --> 02:22:40,388
For video, this process runs not just once but across hundreds of frames, and the model has to ensure that each frame is consistent with the ones before and after it,

1530
02:22:40,878 --> 02:22:44,804
so the motion feels natural and continuous rather than disjoint.

1531
02:22:46,190 --> 02:22:55,588
This is why generating a video is significantly harder than generating an image, and why video generation tools are still maturing faster than image tools.

1532
02:22:56,302 --> 02:23:04,068
And last but not the least, the final output, the final result, is delivered as a polished image or a short video clip,

1533
02:23:04,656 --> 02:23:08,740
realistic, styled, or cinematic, depending on what you asked.

1534
02:23:09,422 --> 02:23:13,060
Four, key features of modern AI image and video tools.

1535
02:23:13,742 --> 02:23:18,148
Here's what makes this generation of tools genuinely powerful for creative work.

1536
02:23:18,958 --> 02:23:19,780
Dual output.

1537
02:23:20,110 --> 02:23:22,916
The best tools handle both images and videos.

1538
02:23:23,438 --> 02:23:26,820
You don't always need separate platforms for different visual formats.

1539
02:23:27,566 --> 02:23:28,260
Style range.

1540
02:23:28,878 --> 02:23:33,028
You can ask for photorealistic outputs that look like professional photographs,

1541
02:23:33,742 --> 02:23:41,936
cinematic shots that feel like movie frames, cartoon or illustrated styles, abstract art, watercolor paintings,

1542
02:23:42,176 --> 02:23:42,596
and more.

1543
02:23:43,406 --> 02:23:46,948
The style is controlled entirely through your prompt understanding.

1544
02:23:48,206 --> 02:23:51,908
These models can interpret highly specific instructions.

1545
02:23:52,814 --> 02:24:01,572
You can describe reflections, time of day, camera angle, movement, weather, and the model will attempt to honor each detail.

1546
02:24:02,382 --> 02:24:05,572
The more precise your prompt, the more tailored your output.

1547
02:24:08,686 --> 02:24:11,652
Many of these tools work within broader AI platforms.

1548
02:24:12,302 --> 02:24:22,436
DAL E3 is built directly into Chat GPT, meaning you can use the same conversation to refine your prompt and generate your image without switching tools.

1549
02:24:23,374 --> 02:24:30,020
If you're not sure how to describe what you want, you can ask ChatGPT to help you write a better visual prompt first.

1550
02:24:31,246 --> 02:24:33,700
When, where can these tools be used?

1551
02:24:34,304 --> 02:24:35,652
Real world applications.

1552
02:24:36,334 --> 02:24:37,732
Let's make this practical.

1553
02:24:38,510 --> 02:24:42,304
Here's where AI image and video generation genuinely adds value.

1554
02:24:42,606 --> 02:24:44,164
First thing is for images:

1555
02:24:44,846 --> 02:24:53,156
creating visual aids and diagrams for educational content, generating illustrations for blogs, books, or presentations,

1556
02:24:53,806 --> 02:24:56,964
producing social media graphics without a graphic designer,

1557
02:24:57,678 --> 02:25:04,036
building product concept mock-ups for early stage ideation, designing thumbnails, banners, or cover art.

1558
02:25:04,910 --> 02:25:14,436
Then comes for videos: creating short explainer, clips for courses or training materials, producing promotional or marketing content for social platforms,

1559
02:25:14,894 --> 02:25:27,300
generating, storytelling visuals for pitches, or proposals, animating concepts that are difficult to explain in words alone, building prototype video content before investing in full whole production.

1560
02:25:28,366 --> 02:25:35,172
The common thread across all of these is you're replacing hours of skilled work with minutes of thoughtful prompting.

1561
02:25:35,790 --> 02:25:37,508
