The arrow shows that orders connected to order items and order items connect to products.

4049
05:50:15,406 --> 05:50:23,140
There's also a neat legend that labels the source database, the ETL layer, the data warehouse, and the BI dashboard.

4050
05:50:23,662 --> 05:50:26,436
The story of the data is now much more clearer.

4051
05:50:26,926 --> 05:50:35,172
If the relationship still doesn't look right, for example, arrows are pointing the wrong way, don't worry, just be even more literal in your prompts.

4052
05:50:35,982 --> 05:50:41,764
Say, draw arrows from customers to orders or from orders to order items to products.

4053
05:50:42,542 --> 05:50:47,716
So with image models, spelling out the direction in plain language often fixes the issues.

4054
05:50:48,398 --> 05:50:52,768
Now further, if I want to modify, I can modify that using the prompt.

4055
05:50:53,262 --> 05:50:58,052
So this time, focus on simplification and tone for a non technical audience.

4056
05:50:58,670 --> 05:51:01,348
Run the generation to produce the simplified version.

4057
05:51:01,966 --> 05:51:04,516
The new image that appears is much cleaner.

4058
05:51:04,910 --> 05:51:13,568
You see the main boxes customers, orders, product, order items, ETL, data warehouse, BI dashboard.

4059
05:51:14,062 --> 05:51:17,540
But inside of each box is empty, just the label.

4060
05:51:18,126 --> 05:51:26,372
The arrow still shows the data flow, but the whole picture looks like the straightforward business diagram you can drop into a slide for a non-technical meeting.

4061
05:51:26,862 --> 05:51:31,076
Now that we are happy with the diagram, let's bring it into our actual client deck.

4062
05:51:31,598 --> 05:51:34,212
